{"tool_name":"tammicol","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"TAMMiCol","home":"https://github.com/HaydenTronnolone/TAMMiCol","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Tool for analysis of the morphology of microbial colonies.","addition_date":"2019-08-11T17:17:43Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:25Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Desktop application"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:TAMMiCol","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"hayden.tronnolone@adelaide.edu.au","name":"H. Tronnolone","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"note":"Tutorial material","type":["Training material"],"url":"https://github.com/HaydenTronnolone/TAMMiCol/blob/master/UserGuide.pdf"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Image analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3443"},{"term":"Post-translation modification site prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0417"},{"term":"Quantification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3799"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"TAMMiCol","operating_system":["Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Ruta","publication":[{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pcbi.1006629","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2018 Tronnolone et al. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.Many microbes are studied by examining colony morphology via two-dimensional top-down images. The quantification of such images typically requires each pixel to be labelled as belonging to either the colony or background, producing a binary image. While this may be achieved manually for a single colony, this process is infeasible for large datasets containing thousands of images. The software Tool for Analysis of the Morphology of Microbial Colonies (TAMMiCol) has been developed to efficiently and automatically convert colony images to binary. TAMMiCol exploits the structure of the images to choose a thresholding tolerance and produce a binary image of the colony. The images produced are shown to compare favourably with images processed manually, while TAMMiCol is shown to outperform standard segmentation methods. Multiple images may be imported together for batch processing, while the binary data may be exported as a CSV or MATLAB MAT file for quantification, or analysed using statistics built into the software. Using the in-built statistics, it is found that images produced by TAMMiCol yield values close to those computed from binary images processed manually. Analysis of a new large dataset using TAMMiCol shows that colonies of Saccharomyces cerevisiae reach a maximum level of filamentous growth once the concentration of ammonium sulfate is reduced to 200 \u03bcM. TAMMiCol is accessed through a graphical user interface, making it easy to use for those without specialist knowledge of image processing, statistical methods or coding.","authors":[{"name":"Binder B.J."},{"name":"Gardner J.M."},{"name":"Jiranek V."},{"name":"Oliver S.G."},{"name":"Sundstrom J.F."},{"name":"Tronnolone H."}],"citationCount":2,"date":"2018-12-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"PLoS Computational Biology","title":"TAMMiCol: Tool for analysis of the morphology of microbial colonies"},"pmcid":"PMC6292648","pmid":"30507938","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Imaging","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3382"},{"term":"Microbial ecology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3697"},{"term":"Protein interaction experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3557"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"TAMMiCol","home":"https://bio.tools/TAMMiCol","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Tool for analysis of the morphology of microbial colonies.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:tammicol/app/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"pac